# Target C: diffuse hull trains give a stronger global full-core surplus floor

Date: 2026-10-04. Base: `419c9bec2ed8fa2cc105a80803a279339e7bea9e`.
Starting state: `2026-09-14.audit.51`. Target: C-II, full-core Type II.
Canonical promotion: `2026-10-05.audit.52` as `C-FULLCORE-HULL-DEPLETION-FLOOR`, after the independent October structural promotion audit.
C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Exact source theorem

Use the actual canonical lexicographic source, retaining all points and contact
isolates. Put

\[
\Delta_2=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3},\quad s=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\quad
\mu_{\rm hull}=\frac{\pi+s}{2}.
\]

For every unbounded full-core Type-II sequence, the new conclusions are

\[
\boxed{\liminf\left(5t_D+\frac{x_B+m_B}{D}\right)
        \ge3\pi+2s},                                      \tag{1}
\]
\[
\boxed{\liminf\left(4\mu_D-\frac{k+3m_B}{D}\right)
        \ge2\pi+2s},                                      \tag{2}
\]
\[
\boxed{\liminf\mu_D\ge\mu_{\rm hull}}.                    \tag{3}
\]

There is no such sequence with `mu_D -> mu_hull`. As in the canonical
endpoint-gap argument, (3) and that exclusion give a non-explicit uniform
positive gap above `mu_hull` for sufficiently large canonical full-core
sources. No value of this gap is claimed.

The implication chain is

```text
near-disk force-hull direction measure + canonical phase deletion
 -> trains with a good hull end carry o(D^2) actual rhombi
 -> at least (2 sqrt(Delta_2)-o(1))D other actual trains
 -> exact source face/pinned ledgers give (1)--(3)
 -> Mantel endpoint extraction excludes mu_D -> mu_hull.
```

This removes the full-core source regime below a substantially larger surplus
floor, not merely a selected grid or shell. It leaves Type I and the higher
surplus regime open. No feasible source is changed or constructed.

## 2. Coordination and independently audited inputs

PR [#666](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/666) appeared during this
run and proposes the diffuse-good-hull-train depletion route. This report
acts as an independent audit of that load-bearing claim and extends its
consequences using the exact good-end count. At the time of the original proof, neither PR #666's report nor any other
unpromoted October result was assumed as a theorem. The present claim is now canonical
through the independent audit.52 promotion.

The [exterior-occurrence proof](target_c_hull_side_exact_train_capacity_audit_2026-10-04.md),
Sections 2--3, is the new dependency proved in this branch. It identifies every
unit loaded-hull side with one good interval. If `m=m_B`, the number of good
terminal occurrences is exactly `q-m`. At most two trains have two good
ends. Therefore the family `G_D` of actual complete stopped trains with at
least one good hull end satisfies

\[
|G_D|\ge q-m-2.                                         \tag{4}
\]

Canonical inputs are Sections 61 and 63 for actual train and face ledgers;
Section 69 for the phase-cut/coarea construction; Sections 68 and 80 for
`R_4/D^2 -> Delta_2`, angular defect `O(D)`, and the force-hull disk limit;
Section 62 and the promoted loaded-chord theorem for the pinned ledger;
Section 75 for `c_I=o(D)`. The final endpoint step uses the already audited
train-family argument in Sections 2--5 of the canonical
[endpoint proof](target_c_fullcore_endpoint_unbalanced_pseudosegment_completion_2026-09-14.md)
and its [independent audit](../audits/target_c_fullcore_endpoint_unbalanced_pseudosegment_completion_independent_audit_2026-09-14.md).
The precise reuse hypotheses are checked in Section 7 below.

## 3. A uniform directional count for unit hull sides

Translate a Type-II sequence so that

\[
B(0,r_D)\subset F_D\subset B(0,R_D),\quad
r_D/D,R_D/D\longrightarrow1/2.
\]

Let `alpha_D=acos(r_D/R_D) -> 0`. At a point `p` of a hull side with
outward unit normal `n`,

\[
n\cdot p\ge r_D,\quad |p|\le R_D,
\quad\angle(n,p)\le\alpha_D.                            \tag{5}
\]

For any oriented normal interval of width `w`, all its sides lie in the
radial-angle interval enlarged by `alpha_D` on each end. Radial
parametrization preserves convex boundary order because `F_D` contains the
inner disk. On a side with support height `h`, its radial function is

\[
\rho(\theta)=\frac{h}{n\cdot e_\theta},\quad
\sqrt{\rho^2+(\rho')^2}
 =\frac{\rho}{\cos\angle(n,e_\theta)}
 \le\frac{R_D}{\cos\alpha_D}.
\]

Integrating over that radial interval bounds the sum of side lengths by

\[
\frac{R_D}{\cos\alpha_D}(w+2\alpha_D).                  \tag{6}
\]

The estimate is uniform in the interval's position; circular intervals that
wrap around the origin are lifted before integration. Each unit side has
length exactly one, so (6) also bounds its count.

A reduced phase bin of width `epsilon<pi/6` consists of three projective
edge-direction intervals. It gives six oriented outward-normal intervals,
each of width `epsilon`. Thus the number of unit hull sides in this union
is at most

\[
\frac{6R_D}{\cos\alpha_D}(\epsilon+2\alpha_D)
=(3\epsilon+o_\epsilon(1))D.                            \tag{7}
\]

This does not assume a rate in the disk limit or a curvature lower bound.
It applies to a polygon with arbitrarily uneven turning. The error is uniform
over bin shifts and over the bin's position.

## 4. Hull-end trains carry only subquadratic rhombi

Fix `epsilon` first. Canonical Section 69 deletes `O_epsilon(D)` actual
rhombus crossings so that each remaining edge has both train potentials in
one real phase bin of width `epsilon`. Every train has one potential and
one of three labels, and its physical projective edge direction equals that
potential plus its label times `pi/3`. No train is duplicated between bins.

Let `T_j` count all train vertices in bin `j`, and let `g_j` count its
vertices in `G_D`. Each member of `G_D` has at least one distinct good hull
side. Equation (7) gives, uniformly in `j`,

\[
g_j\le(3\epsilon+o_\epsilon(1))D.                       \tag{8}
\]

The whole train graph is simple. Therefore the number of retained crossing
edges incident to `G_D` is at most

\[
\sum_j g_jT_j\le(3\epsilon+o_\epsilon(1))D\,T_D.       \tag{9}
\]

The exact canonical identities give `Q=q-x_B-2k<=q`, `f<=Q`, and

\[
T_D=t_DD+2f\le3q\le3\pi D.                             \tag{10}
\]

Adding back the deleted crossings, (9)--(10) imply

\[
\limsup_{D\to\infty}\frac{R_G}{D^2}\le9\pi\epsilon,
\]

where `R_G` counts every actual rhombus having at least one train in `G_D`.
This bound holds for every fixed positive `epsilon`. Taking `epsilon` to
zero **after** the sequence limit proves

\[
\boxed{R_G=o(D^2).}                                     \tag{11}
\]

Only crossing edges of the auxiliary train graph are counted or removed in
this argument. Every source point, contact edge, diameter edge, and actual
rhombus remains in the source. A deleted phase edge is not a deleted packing
constraint. No grain is assumed to share a phase with every other grain.

## 5. Remove good trains before Mantel

Let `M_D=T_D-|G_D|` count the other complete actual trains. Their induced
crossing graph retains

\[
R_{\rm bulk}=R_4-R_G=(\Delta_2-o(1))D^2
\]

edges, by Type II and (11). It is a simple triangle-free graph, so Mantel gives

\[
\boxed{M_D\ge(2s-o(1))D.}                               \tag{12}
\]

Using (4), the exact train count and `f<=Q`,

\[
\begin{aligned}
M_D&\le t_DD+2Q-q+m+2\\
   &=5t_DD-3b+x_B+m+2.                                  \tag{13}
\end{aligned}
\]

Since `b>=P_F` and `P_F/D -> pi`, equations (12)--(13) prove (1).
If `x_B=o(D)`, then `m=o(D)` and

\[
\boxed{\liminf t_D\ge\frac{3\pi+2s}{5}>\frac{2\pi}{3}.} \tag{14}
\]

Thus the whole positive `4pi-6t` one-cell collar window is excluded in the
small-excess branch, not only the October endpoint or two-cell window.

## 6. The unconditional full-core floor

Use `b=q+x_B`, `t_DD=q-k`, and the canonical identity

\[
w_D:=q+m-k=\mu_DD+2c_I+4.                               \tag{15}
\]

Rewriting (13) in terms of `w_D` gives the finite source inequality

\[
\boxed{4\mu_DD+8c_I+18\ge2b+M_D+k+3m.}                 \tag{16}
\]

Indeed its right side is bounded by
`2b+(4q-5k+m-2b+2)+k+3m=4w_D+2`.
There is also an exact slack identity. Let `d_D<=2` count the trains with
two good hull ends. Since `|G_D|=q-m-d_D`,

\[
\boxed{4\mu_DD+8c_I+18-2b-M_D-k-3m
       =2(Q-f)+2-d_D\ge0.}                              \tag{16a}
\]

Divide (16) by `D`, use `c_I=o(D)`, (12), and the perimeter limit. This
proves (2)--(3). All penalty terms are nonnegative because `k=d_H+s_Z>=0`
and `m>=0`. No small-outer-walk or small-matching assumption is used.

## 7. Hostile endpoint audit

Suppose `mu_D -> mu_hull`. Equation (16), `b>=P_F`, and (12) force

\[
M_D/D\to2s,\quad b/D\to\pi,\quad k/D\to0,\quad m/D\to0.
\tag{17}
\]

The induced graph on the `M_D` remaining actual train curves has
`R_bulk/D^2 -> Delta_2`, so its crossing density tends to the Mantel limit:

\[
R_{\rm bulk}/M_D^2\longrightarrow1/4.                    \tag{18}
\]

The canonical endpoint argument, Sections 2--5, uses exactly these geometric
train-family hypotheses, not the formula defining the old surplus floor:

1. actual train curves are pseudosegments with a simple triangle-free crossing
   graph; restriction to a train subfamily preserves this;
2. a phase-circle cut removes only `O(D)` crossing pairs, and the remaining
   trains inherit their same three labels and real potentials;
3. `M_D/D -> 2s` and (18) give Mantel stability on this restricted graph;
4. the retained near-complete two shores have largest distinct-label classes
   of total size at least `3M_D/4-o(D)`, each at least `M_D/4-o(D)`;
5. the canonical unbalanced pseudosegment extraction produces an actual
   complete biclique with the same leading shore sizes;
6. wrong-orientation/equality pairs are the same phase threshold chain graph,
   with `O(D)` edges. Its `O(sqrt(D))` vertex cover removes them;
7. the global `O(D)` angle-defect budget gives a phase-tail trim of `o(D)`
   trains to one bin of width `o(1)`;
8. canonical Section 76 applies to this complete forward actual grid. Its
   endpoints are actual rhombus centers in the full source hull, including
   when other trains, holes, or interfaces occur between selected crossings.

These are precisely the hypotheses checked in the canonical endpoint proof
and its independent audit. Restricting the train vertex set changes none of
the phase equations, curve geometry, or full source-hull diameter. In
particular (11)'s removal of auxiliary train vertices is not a packing
deletion and is not used as a same-cardinality competitor.

For the extracted shores `a_D,b_D`, Section 76 yields

\[
D\ge\frac{\sqrt3}{2}(a_D+b_D)-O(1)
  \ge\frac{3\sqrt3}{4}sD-o(D)-O(1).
\]

The coefficient `3sqrt(3)s/4` exceeds one because
`Delta_2>16/27`. This is the identical strict source-diameter contradiction
in the canonical endpoint proof. Hence (17)--(18) are impossible, and no
full-core sequence converges to `mu_hull`.

If there were no uniform positive gap above `mu_hull`, choose full-core
sources with `D_j>=j` and `mu_j<mu_hull+1/j`. The floor (3) squeezes this
sequence to the forbidden endpoint. This proves the stated non-explicit gap.

## 8. Limits, tests, and remaining obligation

The report's universal statements are analytic. The companion
[exact verifier](../../research/verify_target_c_hull_side_train_capacity_2026_10_04.py)
tests the exterior-occurrence dependency on actual finite rational sources,
the slack identities, interval arithmetic for the constants, and scalar
regressions. It does not prove (11), global optimality, or a uniform rate.
The finite runs passed on 383 rational geometry packets and 183,212 integer
depletion ledgers. The latter checks (16a), including the finite constant
and both good-to-good train losses. It omits geometric realization, KKT,
the phase-depletion limit, and source optimality.

The source-level regime `limsup mu_D<mu_hull` and convergence to its endpoint
are eliminated by this candidate proof. The canonical ledger still records
the old promoted floor until independent promotion. The Type-I macroscopic
container remains open. Higher-surplus Type II remains open, including
strong open-cell deep-port/grid and inactive-pair obstructions.

The next global obligation is a strict source-preserving competitor or a
sharp upper currency bound for sources above `mu_hull`. For any proposed
low-surplus alternative, (1) now requires linear `x_B+m_B`; the exact
global walk must be paid, not silently replaced by a harmonic-cell walk.

Promotion recommendation: independently audit (4), the order of limits in
(11), the finite rewrite (16), and the endpoint restriction before any
theorem-state change. No new external theorem is imported: the endpoint
extraction is the existing canonical, independently audited dependency.
